  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 340 PM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nMid level clouds continue to work slowly to the east this afternoon, \nwith otherwise mostly clear skies. Look for increasing cloud cover \ntonight as our next system approaches the region. H5 upper low will \nmove into the Northern Plains on Monday, into an environment \nsupportive of moderate to heavy rain. Mixing ratios range from 10-12 \ng/Kg by Monday afternoon, with a relatively strong theta-e gradient \nboundary stretching from southwest to northeast across the \neastern half of North Dakota. Embedded thunderstorm activity is \nexpected as CAPE values will be in the 300-700 J/Kg range. Areas\nalong an axis from Cooperstown to Grand Forks, to Roseau have \nthe best chances for 1 or more inches of rain (80-90%), with \nslightly lower chances on either side. High temperatures on \nMonday will be in the upper 50s along the International Border, \nwith low 70s in the southern Red River Valley. Heading into \nTuesday, rain gradually moves off to the east, with minimal \nchances for precipitation through the remainder of next week. \nCooler temperatures are expected from Thursday onward, with \nhighs in the 50s to low 60s and lows in the upper 30s to mid \n40s.",
